INT. HOTEL BALLROOM - ARBITRATION - DAY

A rented ballroom. Chandeliers, stacking chairs, a banquet table draped in white. Through the folding partition, wedding music thumps: a bass line, a cheering crowd.

ARBITRATOR HENRIETTA BOSCO, 60s, sits at the head of the table with a legal pad and a stopwatch. She wears reading glasses on a chain and an expression of hard-won patience.

To her left: CLAIMANT DARNELL FITZGIBBON, 40s, in a suit that doesn't quite close, and his attorney, LUCINDA TAN-ROSSI, 30s, with a color-coded binder.

To her right: RESPONDENT GUSTAV HALVERSEN, 60s, silver beard, folded hands, and his attorney, AMBROSE KELLEHER, 50s, with a fountain pen he clicks constantly.

A paralegal, BRIGITTE, 20s, sits at the end with a laptop and a timer.

The wedding bass drops. Everyone flinches.

BOSCO
Mr. Keller... Kelleher. Your opening.

KELLEHER
Thank you, Arbitrator. My client, Mr. Halversen, has operated this ballroom for twenty years.

LUCINDA
And has never once fixed the ceiling.

KELLEHER
I was not finished.

BOSCO
Ms. Tan-Rossi, wait your turn.

LUCINDA
Apologies, Arbitrator. I was moved.

She grins. Darnell elbows her. Halversen studies the chandelier above them. A crystal quivers with the bass.

KELLEHER
The roof has been inspected annually.

DARNELL
It fell on my cousin's wedding cake.

HALVERSEN
(quietly)
It was a small piece of ceiling.

DARNELL
It was the size of a hubcap!

BOSCO
Mr. Fitzgibbon, you will address counsel, not the respondent.

DARNELL
Sorry, Arbitrator.

She lowers her glasses. He sinks into his seat. Brigitte whispers to Lucinda, who nods, flips three tabs, and slides a photograph across the table.

LUCINDA
Exhibit Nine. The cake, post-impact.

Bosco picks up the photograph. She studies it for a long moment. Something almost like a smile crosses her face and is quickly put away.

BOSCO
Mr. Halversen, did you replace the cake?

HALVERSEN
Madame, I replaced the ceiling.

BOSCO
That wasn't my question.

Halversen unfolds his hands. Refolds them. Kelleher stops clicking his pen. A cheer rises from the wedding. A woman's voice over a microphone: "And now, the first dance!"

Everyone at the table turns toward the partition. The music slows to something soft.

BOSCO (CONT'D)
(to the room)
We will take ten minutes.

She stands. The others stand. Darnell, Halversen, the lawyers, all of them drift toward the divider, and no one says why.
